WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.350
For nine seasons, Brian Baumgartner was an
integral member of the stellar comedic ensemble

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.760
of the American version of The Office.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.890
Baumgartner played Kevin Malone, one of Dunder
Mifflin's accountants, who was generally regarded

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.320
as pretty much the dumbest guy in the office.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.661
Unlike some of his former castmates who went
on to star in their own shows or appear in

00:00:16.661 --> 00:00:20.619
blockbuster movies, Baumgartner has kept a
lower profile since The Office went off the

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:21.619
air.

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:24.519
Here's a look at what he's been up to both
onscreen and off.

00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:26.019
Crashed pilots

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:30.839
In the middle of the final season of The Office
in 2013, Baumgartner already had an eye toward

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:34.620
what he'd be working on next: a new comedy
series for ABC.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.260
Like The Office, it was planned as a single-camera
comedy set in a workplace, conceived by Office

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.850
executive producer Greg Daniels and late Parks
and Recreation writer and producer Harris

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.039
Wittels.

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:48.620
Baumgartner was going to play Norman, a middle
manager at an airline trying to turn his life

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.620
around.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.489
Unfortunately, ABC pulled the plug on the
series before a pilot was shot, reportedly

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:56.399
because executives weren't happy with the
writing.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.570
Baumgartner moved from one disappointing pilot
process into another the next year, when he

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.620
landed a role in Amazon's Maker Shack Agency.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.280
The tween-friendly pilot was about a group
of kids with a knack for inventing who build

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.799
stuff to get their classmates out of jams.

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:12.060
Baumgartner was cast as the kids' principal.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.880
Amazon placed the pilot online for viewing
by the general public, but Maker Shack Agency

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.750
wasn't approved for a season-long run.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.979
Guest roles galore

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:24.260
Baumgartner was a work-a-day actor before
he landed a series regular spot on The Office,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.590
and since the show ended, he's returned to
being a busy character actor on TV.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.690
Since 2013, he's landed more than a dozen
guest roles, including playing a pedophile

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:38.210
on Law &amp; Order: Special Victims Unit, and
a lapsed AA sponsor on the FX drama The Bridge.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:43.590
He's also done a few commercial spots, including
teaming up with a barbershop quartet for Microsoft.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:48.460
Baumgartner has also popped up in several
small films, including 2016's Ordinary World,

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.140
a comedy about an aging punk rocker starring
Billie Joe Armstrong of Green Day …

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.140
"If there is any damage done to that hotel
room, we will prosecute."

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.980
Family time

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.619
With the rigorous shooting schedule of a network
sitcom behind him, Baumgartner has had more

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:05.789
time to focus on his personal life.

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:09.940
In April 2014, he tied the knot with his longtime
partner, Celeste Ackelson.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.150
The wedding went down in the backyard of Baumgartner's
Los Angeles home, and also reportedly served

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.250
as a mini Office reunion.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.900
About a year later, Baumgartner and Ackelson
welcomed a baby girl.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.110
Baumgartner joked on Instagram that his daughter
"looks forward to catching up on all the old

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.030
episodes of The Office once her eyes adjust
to life."

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.040
Tee time

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.709
Baumgartner got really into golf in his theater
days as a way to decompress while traveling

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:35.890
and performing a grueling eight-shows-a-week
schedule.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.980
Baumgartner told The Daily Beast that golf
was a great way to "get outside," and now

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.400
he's a regular on the celebrity golf tournament
circuit, duffing it up in the Drew Brees Celebrity

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.950
Championship and the American Century Championship.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.260
In 2007, Golf Digest ranked Baumgartner at
no.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.160
49 on its list of "Hollywood's Top 100 Golfers."

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.660
"Anybody can compete, at any level, and it's
about that.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:56.710
Competing with yourself.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:57.710
It's always different.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:58.710
Every day is different.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.000
Every course is different."

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Company man

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.260
Baumgartner is a big sports fan, and he's
a veteran of entertainment history.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.599
He combined the two and started his own media
company, 3 Bees Entertainment — a reference

00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:13.110
to his full name, Brian Bruce Baumgartner.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.140
Among the company's projects are an NBC Sports
feature called Golf Therapy: Life, Lessons

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.629
and the Pursuit of Par and a scripted comedy
for College Humor called Turtle Race.

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:24.629
Poker face

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:28.830
"Casino Night," The Office's second-season
finale, revealed a surprising truth about

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:29.860
Kevin Malone:

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:36.120
"I won the 2002 $2500 No-Limit Deuce-to-Seven
Draw Tournament at the World Series of Poker

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.420
in Vegas.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.730
So, yeah.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.909
I'm pretty good at poker."

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:45.019
While Baumgartner isn't a poker champion like
his character, he is an avid player, often

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:47.660
anteing up for celebrity and charity tournaments.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.900
He told Poker News in 2013 that he did so
well at one of those fundraisers, he won a

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.400
seat in the actual World Series of Poker.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.470
Unfortunately, life did not quite imitate
art — Baumgartner had to give up his seat

00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.470
to shoot the show ...

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.470
"I suck."

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.750
Thanks for watching!

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.230
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.459
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

